"Serengeti National Park - UNESCO World Heritage Centre (https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/156/)\nciteturn8view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn3search0\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 198\nL52: In the vast plains of Serengeti National Park, comprising 1.5 million hectares of savannah, the annual migration of two million wildebeests plus hundreds of thousands of gazelles and zebras - followed by their predators in their annual migration in search of pasture and water – is one of the most impressive nature spectacles in the world. The biological diversity of the park is very high with at least four globally threatened or endangered animal species: black rhinoceros, elephant, wild dog, and cheetah.\nL53: Criterion (vii): The Serengeti plains harbour the largest remaining unaltered animal migration in the world where over one million wildebeest plus hundreds of thousands of other ungulates engage in a 1,000 km long annual circular trek spanning the two adjacent countries of Kenya and Tanzania.\nL54: This spectacular phenomenon takes place in a unique scenic setting of ‘endless plains’: 25,000km^{2} of treeless expanses of spectacularly flat short grasslands dotted with rocky outcrops (kopjes) interspersed with rivers and woodlands. The Park also hosts one of the largest and most diverse large predator-prey interactions worldwide, providing a particularly impressive aesthetic experience.\nL55: Criterion (x):  The remarkable spatial-temporal gradient in abiotic factors such as rainfall, temperature, topography and geology, soils and drainage systems in Serengeti National Park manifests in a wide variety of aquatic and terrestrial habitats. The combination of volcanic soils combined with the ecological impact of the migration results in one of the most productive ecosystems on earth, sustaining the largest number of ungulates and the highest concentration of large predators in the world.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn8view1 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn3search15\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMMMR Management Plan 2020-2029 (https://narok.go.ke/wp-content/uploads/2023/04/MNNR-Management-Plan-Final-February-2023-1_compressed.pdf)\nciteturn8view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 139; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn5search12\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 4964\nL18@P1-2: 2015-2016, 2020 and 2023. Earlier phases of the plan\u0002ning work were supported and co-financed by the Afri\u0002can Wildlife Foundation and the Mara Conservancy.\nL19@P2: iii\nL20@P2: Approval page\nL21@P2: The Narok County Assembly has approved the implementation of this\nL22@P2: management plan for the Maasai Mara National Reserve, 2023 – 2032.\nL23@P2-3: \nL24@P3: iv\nL25@P3: Foreword\nL26@P3: In 2023, the Maasai Mara National Reserve will be celebrating 75 years since its establishment\nL27@P3: back in 1948. It is especially appropriate that this historic milestone coincides with the release\nL28@P3: of this M aasai M ara National Reserve 10-year management plan, the first stakeholder\nL29@P3: developed and approved management plan for the Reserve in more than 40 years.\nL30@P3: The Mara Reserve is at the heart of the Mara-Serengeti ecosystem and home to the Great\nL31@P3: Wildebeest Migration, internationally recognised as one of the New Seven Wonders of the\nL32@P3: World alongside other priceless world heritages such as the Polar Ice Caps and the Grand\nL33@P3: Canyon. For those who know the ecosystem well, this accolade comes as no surprise because\nL34@P3: the migration and the natural ecosystem that supports it is without doubt one of the most\nL35@P3: spectacular and breath-taking natural phenomena on earth.\nL36@P3: However, major threats loom that seriously threaten the long-term viability of the Reserve\nL37@P3: and wider ecosystem. If the Reserve is to survive for another 75 years, we urgently need a\nL38@P3: roadmap that will enable us all to confront the many challenges the Reserve now faces.\nL39@P3: It is for this reason that Narok County is proud to unveil this new MMNR Management Plan.\nL40@P3: Through a highly participatory process involving the local community, national and county\nL41@P3: legislators, the tourism industry, ecologists, scientists and Reserve managers, the plan sets out\nL42@P3: a stakeholder-agreed vision for the future of the Mara Reserve plus, in simple and unambiguous\nL43@P3: terms that all can understand, the means to achieve that vision. In line with the global signifi\u0002cance of the Reserve and the greater ecosystem, the plan sets out a comprehensive agenda\nL44@P3: for strengthening the management of the Reserve, designed to ensure the survival of this\nL45@P3: unique and priceless national heritage, and the continuing generation of optimal economic\nL46@P3: benefits from the Reserve for the people of the Narok County in which the Reserve is located,\nL47@P3: as well as for all Kenyans.\nL48@P3: The Mara Reserve and the greater Mara-Serengeti Ecosystem has had an illustrious and suc\u0002cessful past of which we can all be proud, but with the help of this new management plan, we\nL49@P3: are now embarking on a new future for the Reserve – one which is both in tune with the\nL50@P3: international significance of this natural spectacle, its economic importance to Kenya and\nL51@P3: Narok County, as well as with the rapidly mounting threats to the area. To achieve this bright\nL52@P3: new future in the face of the difficult challenges confronting the Reserve will require both bold\nL53@P3: leadership and committed management. We look forward to the next 75 years of the Reserve\nL54@P3: with optimism and excitement about all that will be achieved as the Reserve continues to fulfil\nL55@P3: its vital role in the conservation of the Mara ecosystem.\nL56@P3: Hon. Johnson Ole Sipitiek\nL57@P3: County Executive Committee Member, Tourism & Wildlife\nL58@P3: Narok County\nL59@P3-4: 21st February 2023\nL60@P4: v\nL61@P4: Acknowledgements\nL62@P4: This Maasai Mara National Reserve Management Plan 2023-2032 is the outcome of a combined\nL63@P4: and concerted effort by the many diverse stakeholders of the Reserve, including the local\nL64@P4: communities living around the Reserve who support and benefit from the Reserve, the tour\u0002ism investors and operators that have made the Reserve one of the world’s finest wildlife\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMEFT Namibia - Ministry of Environment and Tourism NamibiaEtosha National Park (https://meft.gov.na/national-parks/etosha-national-park/217/)\nciteturn8view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn7search0\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 129\nL10: One of the largest national parks in Africa, one of the oldest and is Namibia’s number-one tourist destination. Etosha National Park is home  to 114 large and small mammal species, more than 400 recorded bird species, scores of reptiles and even a fish species. Etosha is the  country’s flagship  park.  The size of the  park  has  been reduced considerably since it was first proclaimed in 1907, but it still remains larger than several European countries.\nL11: The Oshindonga name for the pan was Etotha, meaning ‘the place where no plants grow’, but early European traders, unable to pronounce the name, called it ‘Etosha’. The pan was once part of the massive Lake Kunene fed by the Kunene River, which at some time in the distant past dried up, leaving the current pan system. Newly excavated fossils belonging to marsh-dwelling antelopes such  as sitatunga, lechwe and tsessebe, and a 90- cm long catfish, are testament to much wetter periods.\nL12: Etosha has a proud record of black-rhino conservation, and white rhino were recently re-introduced. The park has also played a major role in the recovery of the endemic black- faced impala. The Etosha Ecological Research Institute attracts scientists from around the world.\nL13: \nL14: Etosha’s waterholes are famous among international tourists for spectacular game viewing and at the Okaukuejo waterhole at night it is possible to see  black rhino, lion and elephant.\nL15: \nL16: cite7†Image L17: \nL18: Park size: 22 935 km²\nL19: Proclamation: 1907\nL20: \nL21: \nL22: \nL23: Natural features: The park is dominated by an expansive salt pan and several smaller pans. Scenic waterholes have abundant game. The veld is flat and open, with the only hills around Halali Rest Camp and in the extreme west of the park.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEtosha National Park | Visit Namibia (https://visitnamibia.com.na/2022/02/etosha-national-park/)\nciteturn8view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn7search2\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 459\nL208: Etosha has a proud record of black-rhino conservation, and white rhino were recently re-introduced. The park has also played a major role in the recovery of the endemic black- faced impala. The Etosha Ecological Research Institute attracts scientists from around the world.\nL209: \nL210: Etosha’s waterholes are famous among international tourists for spectacular game viewing and at the Okaukuejo waterhole at night it is possible to see black rhino, lion and elephant.\nL211: \nL212: Proclamation: Etosha National Park in 1907\nL213: Park size 22 935 km2 (including Kaross and Khoabendes)\nL214: \nL215:   * cite58†Etosha Park brochure L216: \nL217: cite59†Image L218: \nL219: Natural features: The park is dominated by an expansive saltpan and several smaller pans. Scenic waterholes have abundant game. The veld is flat and open, with the only hills around\nL220: Halali Rest Camp and in the extreme west of the park.\nL221: \nL222: Vegetation: Lakes and Salt Pans, Nama Karoo and Tree and Shrub Savannah biomes.\nL223: Vegetation types: Karstveld Pans, Western Kalahari, Mopane Shrubland, Etosha Grass and Dwarf Shrubland, North-Eastern Kalahari Woodlands, Western Highlands, Cuvelai Drainage. African moringa tree (Moringa ovalifolia) at Fairytale Forest, water-thorn (Acacia nebrownii), trumpet- thorn (Catophractes alexandri), mopane (Colophospermum mopane), purple-pod terminalia (Terminalia prunoides).\nL225: Tourism: Game viewing. Bird-watching. Photography. Okaukuejo: Premier waterhole chalets, waterhole, family and bush chalets, double rooms. Camping. Restaurant. Bar, kiosk, shop, post office, swimming pool. Flood-lit waterhole, guided morning, afternoon and night game drives. Halali: Family and bush chalets, double rooms. Camping. Restaurant, bar, kiosk, swimming pool. Flood-lit waterhole, guided morning, afternoon and night drives. Nature walks within the camp. Namutoni: Bush chalets, double rooms.\nL226: Camping. African fusion restaurant, steakhouse, bar, curio shop, jewellers and bookstore within the renovated fort. Swimming pool, flood-lit waterhole. Onkoshi: Low impact, environmentally friendly, only 15 units. No entry without a booking. The western part of the park is restricted to Namibian-registered tour operators only.\nL235: Interactive information centres at Halali, Namutoni and Okaukuejo with specific themes will be developed further. These will include interpretive displays, touch screens, auditoriums and other tourist information. The centre at Namutoni will incorporate a museum. Namibia Wildlife Resorts will develop a fifth camp in western Etosha.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLake Louise and Moraine Lake - Banff National Park (https://parks.canada.ca/pn-np/ab/banff/visit/les10-top10/louise?wbdisable=true)\nciteturn8view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn4search5\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 218\nL56:   * Reservations for cite13†campsites in the Lake Louise area begin in January and shuttle reservations begin in April.\nL57:   * Visitors looking to experience Lake Louise and Moraine Lake throughout the summer should start their planning now. Explore our “cite9†Visiting Lake Louise and Moraine Lake \" page now to plan your transit in advance.\nL58:   * When possible, visitors are encouraged to leave their vehicles at home and take transit from either Calgary, Banff or Lake Louise. cite14†Click here for more information .\nL60: Holidays and weekends are especially busy, including:\nL61: \nL62:   * Victoria Day (May, date varies by year)\nL63:   * Canada Day (July 1)\nL64:   * Heritage Day (first Monday in August)\nL65:   * Labour Day (first Monday in September)\nL66:   * Larch season (weekends in September)\nL67: \nL68: From November through April, Lake Louise is a winter wonderland! We receive more snow than other parts of Banff National Park and are proud to have one of the longest ski seasons in North America.\nL69: \nL70: The busiest weekends during the winter are:\nL71:   * Boxing Day through New Year (December 26 to January 1)\nL72:   * Ice Magic Festival (January, dates vary by year)\nL73:   * Family Day (second Monday in February)\nL74:   * Easter weekend (dates vary by year)\nL75: \nL76: How to get there\nL77: \nL78: There are multiple ways to access Lake Louise and Moraine Lake, including shuttles, transit, biking and travelling by foot. Learn more cite9†here .\nL79: Important information: Moraine Lake is considered backcountry. If you choose to bike there, you must be prepared to be self-sufficient. There is no cell service, Wi-Fi, running water or other services including lighting along the road. Pit toilets are available at the lake. There is cell phone reception is available approximately 5 km away from the lake on Moraine Lake Road.\nL80: \nL81: Things to do\nL82: Lake Louise and Moraine Lake usually melt by late May. Alpine hiking season begins in late June. Ski season begins in mid-November.\nL83: \nL84:   * cite15†Hiking L85:   * cite16†Biking L86:   * cite17†Skiing and Snowshoeing L87:   * cite18†Wildlife watching L88: \nL89: Every destination in the Lake Louise area is beautiful but only some are open year-round. This is when the most popular local destinations are open:\nL90:   * Lake Louise lakeshore (year-round)\nL91:   * Community of Lake Louise (year-round)\nL92:   * Moraine Lake (early-June to mid-October)\nL93:   * Lake Louise Ski Resort (November to May)\nL94:   * Summer Gondola at the Lake Louise Ski Resort (mid-May to mid-October)\nL95: When do the teahouses open at Lake Louise?\nL96: The charming Lake Agnes and Plain of Six Glaciers tea houses are the most popular summer hiking destinations in the Lake Louise area. These privately operated teahouses open mid-May and close after Canadian Thanksgiving in mid-October.\nL97: \nL98: The hikes to the teahouses are not safe from November through mid-May due to avalanche hazard. Do not hike to Lake Agnes or to the Plain of Six Glacier during the winter or spring. In May and June, check the trail report before departing.\nL99:   * cite19†Hiking in the Lake Louise area L100:   * cite20†Winter trails in the Lake Louise area L101:   * cite21†Trail condition reports†www.pc.gc.ca L102: \nL103: Safety cite22†Image†pcweb2.azureedge.net Female grizzly bear #142 near Lake Louise.\nL104: #### Summer\nL105: \nL106: In Canada, national parks enjoy special protections that may differ from other protected places. Please know “what not to do” in a national park.\nL107: \nL108:   * cite23†Park regulations L109:   * cite24†Trail condition reports L110:   * cite25†Wildlife and you L111:   * cite26†Bear safety L112: #### Winter\nL113: \nL114: Walking, snowshoeing or tobogganing? You don’t need to venture far to be in avalanche terrain. November through June, not all sign-posted destinations are safe for travel. Choose your winter destination from a list of designated trails.\nL115: \nL116:   * cite27†Avalanche areas at Lake Louise L117:   * cite24†Trail condition reports L118: Visitor centre cite28†Image†pcweb2.azureedge.net L119: The Lake Louise Visitor Centre offers information from both Parks Canada and Banff Lake Louise Tourism about the mountain national parks, accommodations and activities. It is located in the heart of the Community of Lake Louise. Parking for vehicles and RVs is available in the Samson Mall parking lot. A small grocery store, outdoor store, cafés, book store, post office and bank machine are also available here.\nL120: Lake Louise Visitor Centre\nL121: 201 Village Rd, next to the Samson Mall\nL122: Lake Louise, AB T0L 1E0\nL123: GPS: 51.426275, -116.179304\nL124: \nL125:   * cite29†Hours of operation L126:   * cite30†Brochures and publications L127:   * cite31†Google Maps location - Lake Louise area†www.google.ca L128: \nL129: \nL130: \nL131: Maps of the Lake Louise area\nL132: cite32†Download PDF (1.5 MB)†pcweb2.azureedge.net L133: \nL134: Aerial view of the Community of Lake Louise and lake. cite33†Image†pcweb2.azureedge.net L135: \nL136: * * *\nL137: \nL138: Useful links\nL139:   * cite31†Google Maps location - Lake Louise area†www.google.ca L140:   * cite34†Live webcam from the Lake Louise lakeshore†cams.skilouise.com L141:   * cite35†BanffNow†www.pc.gc.ca L142:   * cite6†RoamTransit.com†roamtransit.com L143:   * cite9†Getting around the Lake Louise area L144: \nL145: Date modified :\nL146:     2026-07-31\nL147: \nL148: cite36†Image: Parks Canada logo L149: \nL150: cite37†Job opportunities L151: \nL152: cite38†Volunteer L153: \nL154: cite39†Partner L155: ## Contact us\nL156: \nL157:   * cite40†Contact Parks Canada L158: ## About the Parks Canada Agency\nL159: \nL160:   * cite41†Mandate and Charter L161:   * cite42†Transparency L162:   * cite43†Message from the President and Chief Executive Officer L163:   * cite44†Indigenous relations at Parks Canada L164:   * cite45†Strategies and plans L165:   * cite46†Reports L166:   * cite47†Terms and conditions L167:   * cite48†News†www.canada.ca L168:   * cite49†National historic sites L169:   * cite50†National parks L170:   * cite51†National marine conservation areas L171:   * cite52†National urban parks L172:   * cite53†Nature and science L173:   * cite54†Culture and history --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nParque Nacional Torres del Paine - CONAF (https://www.conaf.cl/parque_nacionales/parque-nacional-torres-del-paine/)\nciteturn8view6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn4search1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 332\nL141: Planifica tu visita: ingresa al sitio de venta de entradas cite53†Pases Parques†www.pasesparques.cl para obtener información actualizada sobre horarios y condiciones para tu visita.\nL142: Recuerda que la información oficial de Torres del Paine solo está disponible en cite6†CONAF y cite53†Pases Parques†www.pasesparques.cl .\nL143: AVISOS:\nL144: 1. Desde mayo en adelante, el circuito Macizo Paine, también conocido como La O, permanecerá cerrado, inicialmente, hasta el 31 de octubre de 2026. Cualquier modificación a esta fecha se avisará oportunamente a través de los canales oficiales de CONAF.\nL145: ATENCIÓN:\nL146: Chile experimenta un brote de influenza aviar de alta patogenicidad (IAAP). cite78†Conoce aquí medidas de prevención para evitar contagios y la expansión de la enfermedad .\nL148: Unidad  | Parque Nacional Torres del Paine\nL149: --- | ---\nL150: Superficie (ha)  | 227.298,00\nL151: Provincia  | Última Esperanza\nL152: Comuna  | Torres del Paine\nL153: Datos generales  | El Parque Nacional Torres del Paine está ubicado en la Región de Magallanes y Antártica Chilena, provincia de Última Esperanza, comuna de Torres del Paine.\nL161: Fauna silvestre  | Las aves son el grupo más representativo: ciento veintiséis especies, hasta el momento, entre las que destacan: ñandú (Rhea pennata), blanquillo (Podiceps occipitalis), cormorán imperial (Phalacrocorax atriceps), garza cuca (Ardea cocoi), bandurria (Theristicus caudatus), flamenco chileno (Phoenicopterus chilensis), coscoroba (Coscoroba coscoroba), cisne de cuello negro (Cygnus melancoryphus), caiquén (Chloephaga picta), pato anteojillo (Speculanas specularis), cóndor (Vultur grypus), halcón peregrino (Falco peregrinus), cachaña (Enicognathus ferrugineus), lechuza (Tyto alba), carpintero (Campephilus magellanicus) y rara (Phytotoma rara).En cuanto a los mamíferos, algunas de las veinticinco especies presentes son: puma (Puma concolor), chingue (Conepatus humboldtii), zorro colorado o culpeo (Lycalopex culpaeus), zorro gris o chilla (Lycalopex griseus), coipo (Myocastor coypus), guanaco (Lama guanicoe) y huemul (Hippocamelus bisulcus).Además, se observan seis especies de reptiles, tres de anfibios y seis de peces, siendo los más comunes de observar los del género Galaxias.\nL162: Servicios  | El Parque Nacional Torres del Paine cuenta con servicios concesionados de alojamiento, zonas para acampar, navegación, venta de alimentos, cabalgatas y artesanía.Alojamiento: con acceso vehicular en hostería Pehoé, hoteles Lago Grey y Explora.\nL163: Sin acceso vehicular en refugios Paine Grande, Grey, Los Perros y Dickson. Alimentación: zonas de picnic en laguna Azul y Villa Monzino (sede administrativa), venta de abarrotes en refugios y zonas para acampar concesionadas, restaurante en camping Serrano y Pehoe. Hoteles cuentan con servicio de restaurante.\nL164: Existe una cafetería en sector Pudeto.Combustible: para visitas en vehículo, considerar que ni en el parque ni en sus alrededores existen estaciones para venta de combustible.Servicios higiénicos y agua potable: el parque cuenta con servicios higiénicos en las porterías del parque (laguna Amarga, lago Sarmiento, río Serrano) y en los lugares de alta concurrencia que se encuentran en el área del camino vehicular (Pudeto y lago Grey) disponen de agua potable mediante cloradores.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBasis and impressions - Swiss National Park (https://nationalpark.ch/en/about/national-park/)\nciteturn8view7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn4search4\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 153\nL7: ### Particularities of the Swiss National Park\nL8: \nL9: Founded in 1914, the Swiss National Park (SNP) is the oldest national park in the Alps and the largest wilderness area in Switzerland. It covers 170 km^{2} of untouched natural landscape with 100 km of marked cite3†hiking trails .\nL55: Thanks to the vision of far-sighted pioneers, Switzerland’s first national park, the oldest in the Alps, was founded in 1914. According to the International Union for the Conservation of Nature (IUCN), the Swiss National Park belongs to the cite5†highest category†www.iucn.org – the “Champions League” – of strict nature reserves. Such high status carries obligations. Within the Park it is not permitted to leave the marked paths; it is prohibited to pick flowers, mow meadows, kill animals or cut down trees.\nL78: The new cite17†National Park Centre in Zernez offers a vast array of interactive, hands-on displays and activities, making it an ideal place for young and old to visit before or after a walk in the Park.\nL79: \nL80: cite25†Federal Act on the Swiss National Park†www.fedlex.admin.ch L81: \nL82: cite26†Cantonal regulation (in German)†www.gr-lex.gr.ch L83: \nL84: E020 Bild&Text mit Links Element\nL85: \nL86: cite27†Image L88: Fritz and Paul Sarasin, Carl Schröter and Steivan Brunies (a native of the Engadine) – who became the founding fathers of the National Park – were concerned about progressive development in the mountains and increasing industrialisation. To counter this movement, they founded the cite28†Swiss Society for the Protection of Nature†www.pronatura.ch , with the vision of setting aside an area of land where nature could develop without human disturbance. In 1906 Brunies commented that\nL89: > “only by means of a vast project such as this can the last remaining original plant and animal species in our land, in a particular area, be saved.” (S. Brunies, 1906)\nL90: \nL91: The first Federal Chief Forest Inspector Johann Coaz also played an important role in the creation of the first national park in the Alps thanks to his networking with key decision-makers. A summary:\nL92: \nL93: \nL94: \nL95: 1904 – 1914\nL96: As early as 1904, Dr. Bühlmann, member of parliament, proposed that a large reserve should be established. Investigations revealed that the area of the Pass dal Fuorn would be ideal, due to its remoteness and abundant flora and fauna.\nL97: In 1909 the nature preservation pioneers were able to lease the cite29†Cluozza valley from the municipality of Zernez for a period of 25 years. To finance payment of the lease, they founded the Swiss Society for the Protection of Nature, now called cite28†Pro Natura.†www.pronatura.ch L98: An outbreak of foot and mouth disease meant that sheep from Italy could no longer graze on the pastures of the municipality of Zernez and financial losses were incurred. This turn of events greatly assisted subsequent negotiations for additional areas to be included in a national park.\nL99: In 1913 a parliamentary committee from Berne visited the Cluozza valley. They were immediately impressed by the idea of a national park. The following year the first national park in the Alps was founded.\nL100: On 1st August 1914 the Swiss National Park was founded. Its first director was Steivan Brunies, a native of the Engadine from Cinuous-chel.\nL101: \nL102: 1915 – 2000\nL103: \nL104: The first setback came in 1936, when – at the request of the municipality of Scuol – the cite30†Tavrü valley was excluded from the Park.\nL105: In 1957, following a heated campaign, the Swiss people voted in favour of granting a concession to the Engadine Power Company to exploit the water power of the Spöl river. This had long-term consequences on the river’s ecological system.\nL106: \nL107: The former National Park House in Zernez was inaugurated in 1968. Since 2008, this building houses the municipality offices as well as the kindergarten.\nL113: Since 2001 much work has gone into the cite32†Biosfera Engiadina Val Müstair project, wherein the National Park would be designated as the core zone, with the cultivated landscape of Val Müstair and part of the territory of Scuol as a cultivation and development zone.\nL114: \nL115: 2008 saw the inauguration of the cite17†National Park Centre in Zernez. This major project opens up new dimensions in the National Park’s public relations activities.\nL116: In 2014, the Swiss National Park celebrates its 100th anniversary with numerous events and projects.\nL117: \nL118: In 2017 UNESCO officially recognises the cite32†Biosphere Reserve Engiadina Val Müstair .\nL119: \nL120: In 2021, the Swiss National Park receives the label cite33†Park of National Importance†www.bafu.admin.ch from the federal government. From this point onwards, the canton of Grisons and the municipalities in the region contribute to the financing of the National Park alongside the federal government.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSagarmatha National Park (https://www.snp.gov.np/)\nciteturn8view8 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn5search0\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 142\nL49: Sagarmatha National Park extends over an area of 1,148 square kilometers of the Himalayan ecological zone in Khumbu region of Nepal. The Park includes the upper catchments areas of the Dudhkoshi and Bhotehoshi Rivers and is largely composed of rugged terrain and gorges of the high Himalayas, ranging from 2,845m at Monjo to the top of the world's highest peak- Sagarmatha at 8,848m above the sea level. Other peaks above 6,000m are Lhotse, Cho Oyu, Thamserku, Nuptse, Amadablam and Pumori.\nL50: The famed Sherpa people, whose lives are interwoven with the teachings of Buddhism, live in the region. The renowned Tengboche and other monasteries are common gathering places to celebrate religious festivals such as Dumje and Mane Rumdu. Tengboche, Thame, Khumjung and Pangboche are some other famous monasteries of this region. For its superlative natural characteristics, UNESCO has enlisted SNP as a World Heritage Site in 1979.\nL52: ##### Sushma Rana\nL53: \nL54: ##### Senior Conservation Officer\nL55: \nL56: ###### +9779801500120\nL57: \nL58: ##### Bibek Baiju\nL59: \nL60: ##### Information Officer & Conservation Officer (Buffer zone and Monitoring Section)\nL61: \nL62: ###### 9801500124\nL63: \nL64: Highlights 26 October, 2024\nL65: \nL66: ### cite7†घोडा सम्बन्धी सूचना L67: \nL68: cite11†NAMCHE BAZAAR WEATHER†forecast7.com L69: \nL70: cite12†Image L71: \nL72: ### cite13†Annual Report 2081/082 L73: \nL74: cite14†Image L75: \nL76: ### cite15†Annual Report 2080/081 L77: \nL78: cite16†Image L79: ### cite17†Status, Distribution and Threats of Himalayan Monal (Lophophorus impejanus) in Sagarmatha National Park L80: \nL81: cite18†Image L82: \nL83: #### Sagarmatha National Park\nL84: \nL85: ###### Sagarmatha National Park was established in 19 July 1976\nL86: \nL87: cite19†Read More†snp.gov.np L88: \nL89: ## Places\nL90: ### Mount Everest\nL91: \nL92: Everest.mountain which lies in Sagarmatha National Park.Every year many mountaineers come from all over the globe to climb this world highest peak of altitude 8,848.80 m (29,030 ft), Every one knows that first of all Sir Edmund Hillary and Tenzing Norgay climbed Mount Everest in 1953 AD.\nL93: \nL94: cite20†Read More L95: #### cite21†Most Downloaded Documents L96: \nL97:   * cite22†SNP Annual Report 2074-2075 L98:   * cite23†SNP Management Plan 2016-2020 L99:   * cite24†SNP Annual Report 073-74 L100:   * cite25†SNP Annual Report 2072-073 L101: \nL102: cite21†View More L103: \nL104: #### cite26†Most Downloaded Legislations L105: \nL106:   * cite27†CITES Act, 2073 L107:   * cite28†राष्ट्रिय निकुन्ज तथा वन्यजन्तु संरक्षण ऐन २०२९ L108:   * cite29†हिमाली राष्ट्रिय निकुन्ज नियमावली २०३६ L109:   * cite30†नेपालको संविधान २०७२ L110: \nL111: cite26†View More L112: #### Photo Gallery\nL113: \nL114: World Wetland Day 2026\nL115: \nL116: cite31†View More L117: \nL118: cite32†iframe†www.youtube.com L119: \nL120: cite33†Image L121: \nL122: #### About Us\nL123: \nL124: Sagarmatha National Park was established in 19 July 1976, covering an area of 1148 km2 in Solukhumbu district in Eastern Development Region of Nepal.\nL125: \nL126: #### Links\nL127: \nL128:   * cite34†Home L129:   * cite35†Department of National Parks and Wildlife Conservation†dnpwc.gov.np L130:   * cite36†Ministry of Forests and Environment†mofe.gov.np L131:   * cite37†Contact "
